  <h1 class="mb-5 mt-0 font-serif text-4xl font-semibold leading-tight text-base-content sm:text-[2.8rem]">Methodology</h1>

  <div class="answer-first">
    <span class="af-label">In short</span>
    <p>The homepage starts with operators that have the most comparable attributes. Sorting a column puts the strongest supported value first, regardless of other missing fields. These are dated operator-published terms, not measured payout results. Commercial relationships do not affect ordering.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="published-offers">Published-benefit benchmark scores</h2>
  <p>The legacy score below remains in the benchmark data for continuity. It no longer determines the homepage order.</p>
  <p>Methodology version: <strong>published-benefits-1</strong>. The three equally weighted components are immediate no-purchase signup Sweeps Coins, immediate SC per dollar in a complete purchase package, and the lowest published cash redemption minimum. There is no purchase-price cap in the overall score; the full package price is always shown.</p>
  <p>Each component scores 100 multiplied by the number of comparable operators with equal or worse values, divided by the number with a comparable value. Higher signup and package values are better; lower cash minimums are better. The overall score is the mean of the three component scores. Scores are relative to covered operators, not a percentage cash return or a claim of market-wide completeness.</p>
  <p>An explicitly absent benefit or a reviewed disclosure gap earns zero for that component, without claiming that a missing amount is numerically zero. We do not redistribute its weight. Overall scores require a supported value or completed absence review for every component. Ties use disclosure completeness, then operator name. A known lack of an offer counts as disclosure; collection failure does not.</p>
  <p>Every bonus benchmark has its own eligibility criteria. Missing redemption information does not exclude an operator from a signup comparison. Staged totals, first-purchase conditions, full purchase prices and gift-card thresholds remain distinct. A no-purchase claim requires explicit support. General thresholds count as cash only when the source explicitly connects them to cash.</p>
  <h2 id="homepage-order">The homepage toplist</h2>
  <p>The homepage contains one list with four comparison attributes: welcome offers, daily rewards, published processing times and cash minimums. Row numbers are list positions, not measured payout ranks. The ordering version is <strong>single-toplist-2</strong>.</p>
  <p>The default order puts the most complete records first, counting each comparable numeric attribute once. A first daily claim, generic reward advertisement, Gold Coins, an unspecified amount and a cash minimum without a confirmed cash method do not count as recurring daily SC or verified cash data. This is information coverage, not a trust score. Missing information lowers default placement without claiming an operator has no benefit.</p>
  <p>Ties in coverage use welcome value, daily SC, processing time, cash minimum, then name. Welcome offers compare immediate free SC first, highest first; paid packages follow, ordered by SC per dollar. The displayed paid package costs $20 or less where available, otherwise its full price remains visible. Sorting any column ignores coverage and uses that attribute alone, with unknown or non-comparable values last in both directions. Ties retain default order.</p>
  <p>Daily sorting uses supported recurring no-purchase SC, not a first claim or staged welcome total. Processing sorting uses the upper end of an explicit processing or approval window, lowest first; nominal days are converted to 24-hour units for ordering. Business-day and calendar-day labels remain visible: this is a comparison of published windows, not predicted arrival dates. Unspecified day types, business-hour-only windows, minimum waits, delivery-only windows and end-to-end claims do not enter the processing sort. Sources and qualifications remain available for each figure.</p>
  <h2>Daily maintenance</h2>
  <p>The monitor is scheduled daily at 06:17 UTC. Configured comparison sources run before general discovery, so core reward and redemption pages are attempted within the bounded request budget. Remaining sources rotate by oldest check. Each run reports comparable attributes and missing fields separately from page-fetch success. A daily run does not guarantee a fresh value for every field. Failed access retains the previous dated evidence.</p>
  <p>Last checked records the attempted source check. First observed records the first capture of a value. Change observed records a difference from the previous extracted value and its qualifying conditions; an initial observation is not a change. Rechecking an unchanged value does not reset its change date. These are observation dates, not claims about when an operator changed its offer.</p>
  <h2>Missing information and failed checks</h2>
  <p>We check operator homepages, promotions, relevant offers, help pages and linked rules, including rendered pages and PDFs when necessary. A documented absence review is required before missing disclosure affects a score. Blocked pages, failed extraction and unfinished collection are our evidence gaps, not operator transparency findings.</p>
  <p>When collection fails, previously supported evidence retains its observation date. Scores are recalculated from those dated inputs; evidence is not presented as freshly verified. New superseding terms or a documented withdrawal take precedence. Operators with insufficient evidence remain in the homepage list with their known benefits, without an overall score.</p>

  <h2>Funded testing</h2>
  <p>Each test uses a real account, real identity verification and real money. We buy the smallest practical coin package, meet the playthrough requirement, then request a redemption and record timestamps for each stage: request, verification, processing and delivery. Repeat redemptions are measured separately from first redemptions, because verification dominates first-request timing.</p>

  <h2>Status labels</h2>
  <ul class="tick-list">
    <li><b>Not tested.</b> We have not funded an account with this operator. Only published terms are shown.</li>
    <li><b>Pending.</b> A funded test is in progress. No observed figures are published yet.</li>
    <li><b>Completed.</b> At least one funded redemption has completed end to end. Observed figures show the date and payment method.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Measured payout rankings</h2>
  <p>We do not rank tested payout speed or reliability until at least three comparable funded tests have completed per operator: comparable means the same redemption type (first or repeat) and, where possible, the same payment method. Published-offer rankings do not imply that those tests have occurred.</p>

  <h2>Sources and claims</h2>
  <p>Every factual claim in an article traces to a listed source with an access date. Operator terms change frequently; when a terms-of-service document lists an effective date, we record it. If we cannot verify a claim, we either remove it or label it as unverified.</p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>Material corrections are recorded with a date in the relevant article's change log when one is present. Minor edits such as typos and formatting changes are not logged.</p>
